    "evidence_text": "from Wikipedia articles, and Task 2 was speculation scope resolution from the BioScope Corpus.\n\nThis subtask of natural language processing, along with another similar subtask, negation detection and scope resolution, have been the subject of a body of work over the years.\n\nWe\n\nFor cue detection, the results are reported for the Average method only, while we report the scores for both Average and First Token for Scope Resolution.\n\ncue detection and scope resolution when trained on a single dataset.\n\nFigure FIGREF11 contains the results for speculation detection and scope resolution for models trained jointly on multiple datasets.\n\nFigure FIGREF14 contains results for negation cue detection and scope resolution.\n\nMost benchmark tasks are sentence classification tasks, whereas the subtasks we experiment on are sequence labelling tasks.\n\nOn the speculation\n\nWhile training on multiple datasets, we observed a gain of 10.6 F1 points on BF and 1.94 F1 points on BA on the speculation detection task and 2.16 F1 points on BF and 0.25 F1 points on SFU on the scope resolution task over the single dataset training approach.\n\nWe thus significantly advance the state-of-the-art for speculation detection and scope resolution.\n\nand speculation detection and scope resolution.",
